  install a pump to remove toxic chemicals  including TCE which the EPA linked  to cancer contaminants don't you know  see signs or see roads and say oh I'll  stop here  Shannon Lisa and Bob Spiegel from the  nonprofit Edison wetlands Association  recently tested the air quality in 14  homes near the former Amphenol site six  tested high for toxins one home and one  outdoor area had levels of TCE  that were more than 18 times Indiana's  corrective action level just the street  separating them there's evidence that  there are potential impacts to these  residential areas far beyond a problem  that the agencies had ever really  explored before some say health and  environmental officials have denied  there's a problem that's why it's  important to voice their concerns at a  recent community meeting I want you to  tell us how you specifically are going  to be more engaged than the people that  were here three years ago and just  patted us on the head and told us to be  quiet we want transparency and we want  honesty for our town at the meeting  state health officials said they've  evaluated cancer numbers and 2015 and  2017 but closed their investigations  when they determined a cancer cluster  did not exist now they say they'll  partner with the CDC to further  investigate so they're actually going to  go back and evaluate all the work that  we've done in the past with regards to  the cancer registry the state's  Department of Environmental Management  offered to retest two of the homes that  tested high for TCE back in the 90s that  we did our sampling we investigated the  site they said they're working out an  action plan for the former Amphenol site  this is kind of starting a little bit  from scratch but using the information  we had in the past about where the  contamination was found my stepson went  through three and a half years of  treatment of he'll Davidson and Reinhart  want the EPA to do comprehensive testing  in the city of Franklin and they say  nothing will stop them our key phrase  right now is not one more not one more  child should have to go through what our  kids have gone through they mess with  the two wrong ones for CBS this morning  Adriana Diaz Johnson County Indiana  there's some strong and angry mothers  well the Amphenol corporation which  owned the land after operations on the  property had ended says it's been  working with the EPA on this for years  it recently conducted a  additional ambient air testing and found  the results met EPA standards the EPA  says it's working with the company on  further testing and the Indiana  Department of Environmental Management  is considering putting a permanent air  monitoring system in Franklin the  mother's we spoke to want hundreds of  local homes tested and when you hear  that story you can understand why
 

